      "response": [
        {
          "value": "You can use `[Environment]::Get-EnvironmentVariable` in PowerShell to retrieve the value of an environment variable. Here’s the basic usage:\n\n```powershell\n# Get the value of an environment variable from the current process\n$value = [Environment]::Get-EnvironmentVariable(\"VARIABLE_NAME\")\n\n# Example: Get the PATH variable\n$path = [Environment]::Get-EnvironmentVariable(\"PATH\")\n\n# To get a variable from a specific scope (Process, User, or Machine):\n$userVar = [Environment]::Get-EnvironmentVariable(\"VARIABLE_NAME\", \"User\")\n$machineVar = [Environment]::Get-EnvironmentVariable(\"VARIABLE_NAME\", \"Machine\")\n$processVar = [Environment]::Get-EnvironmentVariable(\"VARIABLE_NAME\", \"Process\")\n```\n\n**Note:**  \n- If the variable does not exist, it returns `$null`.\n- Scopes: `\"Process\"`, `\"User\"`, or `\"Machine\"`. If not specified, `\"Process\"` is used by default.",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "path": "/c:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        }
      ],
      "responseId": "response_d25e265f-c42c-4422-b931-5398312dc411",
