      "response": [
        {
          "value": "GitHub Copilot and similar AI tools can be influenced by **instruction files** (sometimes called \"system prompts\" or \"configuration prompts\") that provide context, preferences, or rules for code generation. These files are not yet a formal, universally adopted standard, but several approaches are emerging, especially as Copilot expands its customization features.\n\n## 1. What Are Instruction Files?\n\nInstruction files are special files placed in your repository to guide Copilot's behavior. They can:\n- Set coding style or conventions (e.g., naming, formatting).\n- Specify preferred libraries or frameworks.\n- Define documentation or testing standards.\n- Provide context about the project or specific files.\n\n## 2. Current and Emerging Standards\n\n### `.copilot` Directory (Preview Feature)\nGitHub Copilot now supports a [`.copilot` directory](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/configuring-copilot/copilot-workspace-configuration) in your repo, where you can place instruction files such as:\n\n- `.copilot/config.json` — General Copilot configuration.\n- `.copilot/README.md` — Project-wide instructions.\n- `.copilot/{language}.md` — Language-specific instructions (e.g., `powershell.md`, `csharp.md`).\n- `.copilot/{task}.md` — Task-specific instructions (e.g., `testing.md`, `documentation.md`).\n\n**Example:**\n```\n.copilot/\n  README.md\n  powershell.md\n  csharp.md\n  testing.md\n  documentation.md\n```\n\n### File Content Example\n\n#### `.copilot/powershell.md`\n```markdown\n# PowerShell Coding Guidelines\n\n- Use advanced functions with CmdletBinding.\n- Prefer `Write-Verbose` and `Write-Error` for messaging.\n- Use `PascalCase` for function names.\n- Always include comment-based help.\n- Use `param()` blocks for parameters.\n```\n\n#### `.copilot/testing.md`\n```markdown\n# Testing Instructions\n\n- Generate Pester tests for PowerShell.\n- Use descriptive `Describe` and `It` blocks.\n- Mock external dependencies.\n```\n\n#### `.copilot/csharp.md`\n```markdown\n# C# Coding Guidelines\n\n- Use `var` for local variable declarations.\n- Follow Microsoft C# naming conventions.\n- Use XML documentation comments.\n- Prefer async/await for asynchronous code.\n```\n\n## 3. How to Create and Use Them\n\n1. **Create a `.copilot` directory** at the root of your repository.\n2. **Add Markdown files** for each language or task you want to guide Copilot on.\n3. **Write clear, concise instructions** in each file. Use bullet points, code examples, and explicit rules.\n4. **(Optional) Add a general ",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "path": "/c:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        },
        {
          "kind": "inlineReference",
          "inlineReference": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "fsPath": "c:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\\README.md",
            "_sep": 1,
            "external": "file:///c%3A/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/README.md",
            "path": "/c:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/README.md",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        },
        {
          "value": "** in `.copilot` for project-wide guidance.\n\n## 4. Limitations and Notes\n\n- **Feature Availability:** As of mid-2024, these features are in preview and may require enabling in your GitHub Copilot settings.\n- **Scope:** Instructions are hints, not hard rules. Copilot will try to follow them but may not always do so perfectly.\n- **Language/Task Specificity:** Naming your files after languages (`powershell.md`, `csharp.md`) or tasks (`testing.md`, `documentation.md`) helps Copilot apply the right context.\n\n## 5. References\n\n- [Copilot Workspace Configuration (GitHub Docs)](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/configuring-copilot/copilot-workspace-configuration)\n- [Copilot Customization Blog Post](https://github.blog/changelog/2024-01-17-copilot-workspace-configuration-in-public-beta/)\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nYou can guide Copilot by creating a `.copilot` directory with Markdown files named for languages or tasks, containing your instructions. This helps Copilot generate code, docs, or tests that better fit your project's needs.",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
