              "markdownBeforeBlock": "GitHub Copilot and similar AI tools can be influenced by **instruction files** (sometimes called \"system prompts\" or \"configuration prompts\") that provide context, preferences, or rules for code generation. These files are not yet a formal, universally adopted standard, but several approaches are emerging, especially as Copilot expands its customization features.\n\n## 1. What Are Instruction Files?\n\nInstruction files are special files placed in your repository to guide Copilot's behavior. They can:\n- Set coding style or conventions (e.g., naming, formatting).\n- Specify preferred libraries or frameworks.\n- Define documentation or testing standards.\n- Provide context about the project or specific files.\n\n## 2. Current and Emerging Standards\n\n### `.copilot` Directory (Preview Feature)\nGitHub Copilot now supports a [`.copilot` directory](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/configuring-copilot/copilot-workspace-configuration) in your repo, where you can place instruction files such as:\n\n- `.copilot/config.json` — General Copilot configuration.\n- `.copilot/README.md` — Project-wide instructions.\n- `.copilot/{language}.md` — Language-specific instructions (e.g., `powershell.md`, `csharp.md`).\n- `.copilot/{task}.md` — Task-specific instructions (e.g., `testing.md`, `documentation.md`).\n\n**Example:**\n"
